WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:04.770
I'm Jenna Fisher.
我是珍娜·费舍尔。

2
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.070
And I'm Angela Kinsey.
我是安吉拉·金赛。

3
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.370
We were on The Office together.
我们曾一起出演《办公室》。

4
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.610
And we're best friends.
我们是最好的朋友。

5
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:12.430
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.
现在，我们专门为你们制作了这档《办公室》终极重温播客。

6
00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:19.410
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.
每周，我们都会深度解析一集《办公室》，并分享只有身临其境的我们才能告诉你的独家幕后故事。

7
00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:20.590
We're The Office ladies.
我们是《办公室》女士。

8
00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:29.370
Today, you guys.
伙计们，今天。

9
00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:31.330
We're going to the beach today.
我们要去海滩了。

10
00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:33.730
We're going to play some beach games.
我们要玩一些海滩游戏。

11
00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:35.190
Yes.
没错。

12
00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:42.010
Season 3, Episode 22, written by Jen Salata and Greg Daniels, directed by Harold Ramis.
第三季第22集，由简·萨拉塔和格雷格·丹尼尔斯编剧，哈罗德·雷米斯执导。

13
00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:43.330
It's beach games.
这一集是《海滩游戏》。

14
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.230
We have so much to talk about.
我们要聊的内容实在太多了。

15
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.890
I kind of feel like we just got to get to it.
我觉得我们得赶紧开始了。

16
00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:50.710
We should dive in because I took very diligent notes.
我们应该直接切入主题，因为我做了非常详尽的笔记。

17
00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:53.250
And I think you did in real life.
而且我觉得你在现实生活中也做了。

18
00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:54.610
We'll get to that.
我们待会儿会聊到那个。

19
00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:56.010
All right.
好的。

20
00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:56.910
Here's a summary.
这是剧情梗概。

21
00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:01.630
Michael is invited to interview for a job opening at corporate.
迈克尔受邀去公司总部面试一个职位空缺。

22
00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:10.830
Certain that he's a lock for the job, he takes his employees to the beach at Lake Scranton and holds a series of challenges to determine his successor.
他确信自己稳操胜券，于是带着员工们来到斯克兰顿湖，并举行了一系列挑战赛来选拔接班人。

23
00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:15.390
At the end of the day, Michael sets up a cold walk as a final challenge.
在这一天的最后，迈克尔设置了一场“走火炭”作为终极挑战。

24
00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:22.650
Pam does the cold walk and summons up the courage to tell Jim and the whole office her real feelings.
帕姆完成了走火炭，并鼓起勇气向吉姆和整个办公室表达了她的真实感受。

25
00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:24.150
Oh, man.
天哪。

26
00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:28.030
That was so tough to watch.
那一幕看得我太难受了。

27
00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:29.850
It was beautiful, Jenna.
那真的很动人，珍娜。

28
00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:32.790
Pam broke my heart in this episode.
这一集里帕姆让我心碎了。

29
00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:33.790
Yeah.
是啊。

30
00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:36.070
I had not seen this episode since it aired.
自这一集播出后，我就没再看过。

31
00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:40.450
And I, it shocked me.
我……它震惊了我。

32
00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:42.310
And I did it.
而且那是我演的。

33
00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:44.470
And I was still shocked.
但我依然感到震惊。

34
00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:45.770
Isn't that funny?
这难道不有趣吗？

35
00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:48.550
Like the way the brain works where you're like, wait, I did all that.
大脑的运作方式真的很奇妙，你会想：等等，那些都是我做的吗？

36
00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:49.510
That's crazy.
太疯狂了。

37
00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:50.550
That's kind of cool.
感觉挺酷的。

38
00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:51.530
Yeah.
是啊。

39
00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:58.050
So fast fact number one, Angela, our beach games is set on Lake Scranton.
第一个速览事实，安吉拉，我们的《海滩游戏》是在斯克兰顿湖拍摄的。

40
00:01:58.050 --> 00:01:58.950
Yes.
没错。

41
00:01:58.950 --> 00:01:59.050
Yeah.
是啊。

42
00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:03.990
Obviously, we didn't go to the real Lake Scranton, but I did want to just talk a little bit about it.
显然，我们并没有去真正的斯克兰顿湖，但我还是想简单聊聊它。

43
00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:05.210
Oh, let's hear it.
哦，说来听听。

44
00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:13.010
Well, Lake Scranton is actually a reservoir, and it was built with a dam in the early 1900s.
嗯，斯克兰顿湖实际上是一个水库，它是20世纪初通过修建大坝形成的。

45
00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:24.970
The dam was built by William Walker Scranton, and the body of water it created was originally called the Burnt Bridge Reservoir, although the public calls it Lake Scranton.
这座大坝是由威廉·沃克·斯克兰顿建造的，它形成的水域最初被称为“烧桥水库”，尽管公众称之为斯克兰顿湖。

46
00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:32.570
Well, Jenna, you and I have both been to Scranton, and one of the times that I went, they drove me around Lake Scranton, and it is so pretty.
嗯，珍娜，你我都去过斯克兰顿，有一次我去的时候，他们开车带我绕着斯克兰顿湖转了一圈，那里真的很美。

47
00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:33.930
So pretty.
非常美。

48
00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:43.750
There is a running track around the real Lake Scranton, and it's absolutely gorgeous, but I did look up a few facts about it, Ange.
真正的斯克兰顿湖周围有一条跑道，景色绝对迷人，不过我确实查了一些关于它的资料，安吉。

49
00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:44.250
Yeah?
是吗？

50
00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:47.030
Swimming is not permitted.
那里禁止游泳。

51
00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:52.030
Boating is not permitted, but canoeing and kayaking is allowed.
这里禁止划船，但允许划独木舟和皮划艇。

52
00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:53.710
I wonder why you can't swim in it.
我纳闷为什么不能在里面游泳。

53
00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:56.030
Like, I get the no motor, right?
比如，我理解禁止机动船，对吧？

54
00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:56.770
That's like-
那就像是——

55
00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:56.970
Right.
没错。

56
00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:00.990
Like, I think that's sort of really cool, but I wonder why they're no swimming.
我觉得这挺酷的，但我还是想知道为什么不能游泳。

57
00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:02.290
I don't know.
我不知道。

58
00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:02.730
Hmm.
嗯。

59
00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:04.070
I'm not sure.
我不确定。

60
00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:04.890
We're intrigued.
我们很好奇。

61
00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:06.170
Scranton, write us.
斯克兰顿，写信告诉我们吧。

62
00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:07.790
Tell us why you can't swim in your lake.
告诉我们为什么你们的湖里不能游泳。

63
00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:12.250
Well, this leads me to fast fact number two.
好吧，这引出了我的第二个冷知识。

64
00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:23.750
I brought up all these facts about Lake Scranton because we had a fan question from Sophia B., Aaron W., Dana D., Maren B., and Faye B.
我之所以提到这些关于斯克兰顿湖的事实，是因为我们收到了来自Sophia B.、Aaron W.、Dana D.、Maren B.和Faye B.的粉丝提问。

65
00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:27.670
Where was the beach that you filmed this episode?
你们拍摄这一集时的海滩在哪里？

66
00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:32.870
Was it a long commute, and how many days did you film at that location?
通勤时间长吗？你们在那里拍了几天？

67
00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:35.950
Well, I will tell you.
嗯，我会告诉你们的。

68
00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:37.250
Tell us.
告诉我们吧。

69
00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:38.350
I know.
我知道。

70
00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:42.690
I remember one or two things, but I bet you got all the details, so let's hear it.
我记得一两件事，但我敢说你掌握了所有细节，所以让我们听听吧。

71
00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:45.890
Well, I reached out to Kentopedia, and here's what he told me.
好吧，我联系了Kentopedia，这是他告诉我的。

72
00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:51.190
We filmed at the beach at the Hanson Dam Recreation Center in Sylmar.
我们在希尔马的汉森大坝娱乐中心的海滩拍摄的。

73
00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:54.050
We shot there for four days and nights.
我们在那里拍了四个昼夜。

74
00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:57.190
Well, you mentioned that you've been to the real Lake Scranton.
嗯，你提到过你去过真正的斯克兰顿湖。

75
00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:03.350
I've been to this real Hanson Dam Recreation Center outside of us filming.
除了拍摄之外，我也去过这个真正的汉森大坝娱乐中心。

76
00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:04.150
You have?
你去过？

77
00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:11.210
Yes, because there is this museum, this children's museum called the Discovery Cube that's right nearby.
是的，因为附近有一个儿童博物馆，叫探索立方体。

78
00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:17.210
And when you go through this area, there's also like an equestrian center.
当你经过这个区域时，还有一个马术中心。

79
00:04:17.210 --> 00:04:20.090
There's an aquatic center, there's a playground.
那里有水上中心，还有游乐场。

80
00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:30.070
Because, lady, the place that they put us to film this episode was like you had – I had no idea all this stuff was there.
因为，女士，他们安排我们拍摄这一集的地方，我根本不知道那里有这么多东西。

81
00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:39.670
My memory of where we filmed was it was like a chemical pond runoff from a dam where I was like, are we breathing in fumes?
我对拍摄地点的记忆是，它就像大坝排出的化学废水池，当时我在想，我们是不是在吸入废气？

82
00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:42.670
It was really like gross.
真的感觉很恶心。

83
00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:53.750
They – maybe like the only place they let you film is in this like weird janky corner of this area because –
他们——也许他们唯一让你拍摄的地方就是这个区域里一个奇怪又破烂的角落，因为——

84
00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:55.290
Where the runoff water goes.
那是废水流向的地方。

85
00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:59.510
Yeah, because this recreation center is actually really nice.
是的，因为这个娱乐中心其实非常好。

86
00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:02.590
But here's something I found out too.
但我也发现了这一点。

87
00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:06.730
The lake where we filmed is filled with circulated drinking water.
我们拍摄的那个湖里装的是循环饮用水。

88
00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:07.510
What?
什么？

89
00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:12.190
Yeah, circulated drinking water and it offers fishing and public boating.
是的，循环饮用水，而且提供钓鱼和公共划船项目。

90
00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:14.450
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

91
00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:20.230
So, when Ed is out there floating in the water, he was in some recycled drinking water.
所以，当艾德在水里漂着的时候，他是在循环饮用水里。

92
00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:21.710
Oh, well, that's good to know.
哦，那真是好消息。

93
00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:25.850
All these years I worried for him that he was going to get like some kind of weird rash.
这么多年来，我一直担心他会染上什么奇怪的皮疹。

94
00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:30.650
All right.
好吧。

95
00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:33.070
Well, let's move on to fastback number three.
那么，让我们进入第三个冷知识。

96
00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:44.210
So, like we said earlier, this was written by both Greg Daniels and Jen Salata and we reached out to Jen Salata and she sent us audio clips.
正如我们之前所说，这一集是由格雷格·丹尼尔斯和珍·萨拉塔共同编写的，我们联系了珍·萨拉塔，她发给了我们音频片段。

97
00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:45.830
She sent us so many.
她发了好多。

98
00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:47.150
She's so gracious.
她真是太慷慨了。

99
00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:48.850
We're going to sprinkle them throughout.
我们将把这些片段穿插在节目中。

100
00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:51.370
But here she has one for us right now.
但她现在就有一个片段要给我们。

101
00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:59.570
Yes, she is going to tell us a little bit about the inspiration for this episode and what it was like to write a script with Greg.
没错，她会跟我们聊聊这一集的创作灵感，以及和格雷格一起写剧本是什么样的体验。

102
00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:04.150
I think the inspiration for this episode was just Survivor.
我觉得这一集的灵感来源就是《幸存者》节目。

103
00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:05.870
I think it was Greg's idea.
我想这应该是格雷格的主意。

104
00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:13.590
And I remember all of us talking about Survivor being a perfect way for Michael Scott to pick his successor.
我记得我们当时都在讨论，《幸存者》这种形式简直是迈克尔·斯科特挑选接班人的绝佳方式。

105
00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:20.670
I mean, what other way would this man use to try to pick somebody who could run a branch?
我是说，除了这种方式，这个男人还能想出什么别的法子来选出能管理分公司的人呢？

106
00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:25.330
It just seemed like a no-brainer for Michael Scott to do it this way.
对迈克尔·斯科特来说，用这种方式简直是显而易见的选择。

107
00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:30.050
And then we tried to sit down and write the whole thing together and we were like, this isn't going to work.
后来我们试着坐下来一起写完整剧本，结果发现根本行不通。

108
00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:31.350
So we divided it up.
所以我们分工合作了。

109
00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:35.090
We can't remember exactly how we did it.
我们记不清具体是怎么分的了。

110
00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:40.210
I think sometimes with Paul, when I write a script with Paul, I do the first half, he'd do the second or vice versa.
我觉得有时和保罗写剧本时，我会写前半部分，他写后半部分，反之亦然。

111
00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:44.850
And with Greg, I think we didn't do cleanly divide it up that way.
至于格雷格，我觉得我们当时并没有那样明确地分工。

112
00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:47.430
I think just he did some of the scenes, I did others.
我想就是他写了一些场景，我写了另一些。

113
00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:53.070
And I do remember that the Pam speech was mostly Greg.
我确实记得帕姆的那段演讲主要是格雷格写的。

114
00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:56.350
And I think that is such a beautiful part of the episode.
我觉得那是这一集中非常精彩的一部分。

115
00:06:56.590 --> 00:07:01.950
And the Pam arc preexisted the idea of the Beach Games episode.
而且帕姆的故事线在“海滩游戏”这一集构思出来之前就已经存在了。

116
00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:06.290
So I think some of the ideas from that speech were in his notes.
所以我认为那段演讲里的一些想法当时就在他的笔记里了。

117
00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:08.650
But I love that part.
但我很喜欢那部分。

118
00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:11.790
It is one of my favorite parts, if not my favorite part of the episode.
那是我这一集中最喜欢的片段之一，甚至可以说是最喜欢的部分。

119
00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:14.590
So credit to Greg for that part for sure.
所以那部分绝对要归功于格雷格。

120
00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:16.390
But we both wrote a bunch of it.
不过我们俩确实都写了很多内容。

121
00:07:16.390 --> 00:07:18.650
I just don't think we clearly divided it up.
我只是觉得我们没有进行明确的分工。

122
00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:27.110
Well, Ange, I remember Greg talking to me about this Pam arc of her finding her voice.
嗯，安吉，我记得格雷格跟我聊过帕姆这条找回自我的故事线。

123
00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:27.450
Yeah.
没错。

124
00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:32.930
And he planted this idea a few episodes ago and we sprinkled it in.
他在几集之前就埋下了这个伏笔，我们之后也零星地穿插了一些内容。

125
00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:39.010
And so I just love this idea that he'd been maybe writing down little notes about what Pam will
所以我特别喜欢这个构思，他可能一直在记些小笔记，记录帕姆

126
00:07:39.010 --> 00:07:41.050
finally say when she says something.
最终说出心里话时会讲些什么。

127
00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:48.570
And I remember getting the script for this episode and just being so excited as an actor.
我记得拿到这一集剧本时，作为演员我感到非常兴奋。

128
00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:50.530
There was just so much to chew on.
里面有太多值得细细品味的东西了。

129
00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:55.330
I mean, I thought that it was just beautifully done.
我是说，我觉得这写得太棒了。

130
00:07:55.330 --> 00:08:03.010
Really, just Pam's whole journey this season about even her sending the wrong beer back.
真的，帕姆这一季的整个心路历程，甚至包括她退回那杯错误的啤酒。

131
00:08:03.230 --> 00:08:03.930
You know what I mean?
你明白我的意思吗？

132
00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:08.470
And just having these moments where she stood up for herself and spoke her truth.
就是那些她为自己挺身而出、说出真心话的时刻。

133
00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:11.370
And it all builds to this one moment.
这一切都汇聚到了这一个瞬间。

134
00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:13.770
And it's just so well done.
真的写得太好了。

135
00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:20.590
When I was re-watching this, I was marveling at what a well-written episode this was.
重看这一集的时候，我惊叹于这集剧本写得有多么出色。

136
00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:21.610
Yes.
没错。

137
00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:27.290
Well, maybe we should take a quick break and then come back and break down this episode.
好吧，也许我们该休息一下，回来后再深入剖析这一集。

138
00:08:27.410 --> 00:08:28.090
I can't wait.
我等不及了。

139
00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:29.850
Jenna, we have so much to talk about.
珍娜，我们要聊的话题实在太多了。

140
00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:30.870
I know.
我知道。

141
00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:33.070
We've been trading a lot of texts about this one.
关于这一集，我们已经互发了很多信息讨论了。

142
00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:42.770
All right.
好的。

143
00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:45.650
So this episode starts with this very funny cold open.
这一集是以一个非常有趣的开场戏开始的。

144
00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:47.970
It is Dwight and Michael.
是德怀特和迈克尔。

145
00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:49.330
They're in Michael's office.
他们在迈克尔的办公室里。

146
00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:51.990
And Michael's convinced that he's sick, you guys.
迈克尔坚信自己生病了，伙计们。

147
00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:52.850
He's convinced.
他非常确信。

148
00:08:53.090 --> 00:08:55.350
They're scrolling through a website.
他们在浏览一个网站。

149
00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:59.870
It's like WebMD or something, trying to figure out what he is sick from.
就像是WebMD之类的网站，试图查出他到底得了什么病。

150
00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:01.130
Dwight's very concerned.
德怀特非常担心。

151
00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:02.310
Yes.
是的。

152
00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:06.430
Pam has a talking head where she explains that this happens about 40 times a year.
帕姆有个采访镜头，解释说这种情况一年大约会发生40次。

153
00:09:07.450 --> 00:09:09.850
Michael, it's sick but has no symptoMs.
迈克尔，这病很严重，但没有症状。

154
00:09:10.330 --> 00:09:10.870
Yes.
是的。

155
00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:15.430
Well, Jen left us a really great audio clip about this, about the inspiration for this
嗯，简给我们留了一段非常棒的音频，讲了关于这个开场戏的灵感来源。

156
00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:16.030
cold open.
开场戏。

157
00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:17.370
Sam, can you play that?
萨姆，你能播放一下吗？

158
00:09:17.650 --> 00:09:20.810
Greg and I wrote the episode together and it was really fun.
格雷格和我一起写了这一集，过程非常有趣。

159
00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:25.510
I remember being really curious, what is Greg's process going to be like?
我记得当时很好奇，格雷格的创作过程会是什么样的？

160
00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:29.210
Because writing scripts, it was such a solitary part of what we did.
因为写剧本是我们工作中非常孤独的一部分。

161
00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:33.750
And so I was always wondering, what are other writers like when they go off to write a script?
所以我一直很好奇，其他编剧在去写剧本时是什么样的？

162
00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:39.830
It became quickly obvious that his process at the beginning was like mine, which is like
很快就发现，他一开始的过程和我一样，就像我见过的所有其他编剧一样，

163
00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:44.170
every other writer I've ever met, which is there's a fair amount of procrastinating right
在必须开始艰苦工作之前，总会有相当长一段时间的拖延，

164
00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:45.950
away before you have to get into the hard work.
马上就开始了。

165
00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:47.670
It's figuring out what you want to eat.
就是琢磨想吃什么。

166
00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:48.950
It's sharpening your pencils.
就是削铅笔。

167
00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:51.190
It's figuring out the temperature of the room.
就是调整房间的温度。

168
00:09:52.270 --> 00:10:00.090
And for both of us, we started focusing on ailments, like what was wrong, how we weren't feeling
我们俩都开始关注身体不适，比如哪里不对劲，感觉哪里不舒服，

169
00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:03.870
well, like my ear was bothering him and he wasn't feeling super well at the time.
比如我的耳朵让他感到困扰，而他当时也感觉不太好。

170
00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:07.890
And so we went on WebMD and we tried to diagnose ourselves and each other.
于是我们上了WebMD网站，试图给自己和对方诊断。

171
00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:14.490
And they used to have a little body where you could like put point to the part of the body
他们以前有个小人模型，你可以指着身体上疼痛或让你困扰的部位，

172
00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:15.890
that hurt and that was bothering you.
然后进行诊断。

173
00:10:16.030 --> 00:10:21.330
And so he diagnosed me with an ear infection or at least inner ear congestion.
他诊断我得了耳部感染，或者至少是内耳充血。

174
00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:27.230
And he ended up putting some oil from the kitchen that we found in my ear.
最后他从厨房找了点油滴进我的耳朵里。

175
00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:29.510
It turns out you can do this.
事实证明这招确实管用。

176
00:10:29.510 --> 00:10:35.570
Um, it is, it is a home remedy and it helped, it helped my ear.
嗯，这是一种家庭疗法，确实对我的耳朵有帮助。

177
00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:38.090
And I think we diagnosed him with walking pneumonia.
我想我们当时诊断他得了游走性肺炎。

178
00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:43.850
So we did this right when we were sent off to write the beach games episode and it ended
所以我们刚被派去写《海滩游戏》那一集时就做了这件事，最后它成了我们的开场戏，

179
00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:49.770
up becoming our cold open because we were probably looking for a cold open and we're like, what
因为我们当时可能正在找开场戏的灵感，然后我们想，我们现在做的这些事怎么样？

180
00:10:49.770 --> 00:10:51.890
about what we're doing right now?
关于我们现在正在做的事情？

181
00:10:51.890 --> 00:10:59.570
Oh, there is so much about that audio clip that I love.
哦，那段音频里有很多我喜欢的内容。

182
00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:01.110
I hit so relatable.
太有共鸣了。

183
00:11:01.370 --> 00:11:04.910
It's like whenever you sit down to do something and it doesn't matter what it is.
就像无论你坐下来要做什么，不管是什么事情都一样。

184
00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:09.030
I mean, there's was this writing assignment, but all the different ways you procrastinate
我是说，当时是有这个写作任务，但在你开始做那件事之前，

185
00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:10.730
before you get to that thing.
你会用各种各样的方式来拖延。

186
00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:11.610
Yes.
是的。

187
00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:15.950
Well, Angela, you and I are working on a project that we can't say exactly what it is,
安吉拉，你和我正在做一个项目，虽然不能说具体是什么，

188
00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:24.110
but it involves quite a bit of writing and I, every time I sit down to write, I will
但它涉及大量的写作，我每次坐下来写作时，

189
00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:28.950
write one sentence and then I will get up and get a snack and then I'll come back and write
写上一句，然后就会起身去拿零食，回来后再写上一句，

190
00:11:28.950 --> 00:11:30.970
a sentence and then I'll be like, I need more coffee.
然后又会觉得，我需要再来杯咖啡。

191
00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:34.330
It's like the procrastination.
这就是拖延症。

192
00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:41.190
I loved that she shared that because I think my perception is always that creativity comes
我很喜欢她分享这些，因为我觉得我的认知里，成功人士的创造力总是来得很轻松。

193
00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:43.510
easy to people who are successful.
我总觉得那一定是源源不断地涌现出来的，但实际上那是一个非常艰苦的过程。

194
00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:48.130
I always think like it must pour out of them and it is such a slog.
所以我很喜欢听到这些。

195
00:11:48.350 --> 00:11:49.730
So I loved hearing that.
所以我很喜欢听到这些。

196
00:11:49.870 --> 00:11:57.130
But then the other thing I loved was that their procrastination, it got turned into a story
但另一件我喜欢的事是，他们的拖延竟然变成了一个故事创意。

197
00:11:57.130 --> 00:11:57.630
idea.
这很值得。

198
00:11:57.810 --> 00:11:58.890
Like it's worth it.
就像所有那些胡闹的事情，最终产出了成果。

199
00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:02.790
Like all of that nonsense, it produced something.
就像所有那些胡闹的事情，最终产出了成果。

200
00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:03.430
Right.
对吧。

201
00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:07.850
Well, I had a writing professor in college that used to always say, write what you know,
我大学时有一位写作教授总是说，写你所熟悉的，

202
00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:10.050
write what you know and it will be honeSt.
写你所熟悉的，这样才会真诚。

203
00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:14.830
Oh, well, I just loved this, this opening.
噢，我太喜欢这个开场了。

204
00:12:15.530 --> 00:12:21.650
And, you know, before they can really diagnose Michael, Pam walks in the office and says,
而且，在他们能真正给迈克尔下诊断之前，帕姆走进办公室说，

205
00:12:21.830 --> 00:12:23.130
David Wallace is on the phone.
大卫·华莱士在电话上。

206
00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:23.670
Yeah.
是啊。

207
00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:27.510
It's very clear that David Wallace does not call very often.
很明显，大卫·华莱士并不常打电话来。

208
00:12:27.890 --> 00:12:29.030
No, it's a big deal.
没错，这可是件大事。

209
00:12:30.310 --> 00:12:32.370
Well, Michael gets on the phone with him.
迈克尔接了他的电话。

210
00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:33.850
Oh my goodness.
天哪。

211
00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:35.690
Michael, what are you doing?
迈克尔，你在干什么？

212
00:12:36.410 --> 00:12:44.550
Michael, the way Michael talks to his bosses or even when he goes to New York to corporate,
迈克尔，迈克尔跟老板说话的方式，甚至当他去纽约总部时，

213
00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:50.970
there is such a casualness to how he relates to them that is so not work appropriate.
他与他们交流时那种随意劲儿，简直太不符合职场规范了。

214
00:12:53.050 --> 00:12:54.910
It's so true.
确实如此。

215
00:12:55.410 --> 00:13:00.950
Well, we find out the reason that David Wallace is calling is because there is a job opening at
我们发现大卫·华莱士打电话的原因是总部有一个职位空缺，

216
00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:03.130
corporate and he would like Michael to apply.
他想让迈克尔申请。

217
00:13:03.130 --> 00:13:08.290
I can't believe he still wants Michael to apply after this phone call, but I think Michael has a
真不敢相信在通完这个电话后，他竟然还想让迈克尔申请，但我认为迈克尔的

218
00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:16.510
very, very good sales record and a good record of connecting with people out in the sales world.
销售业绩非常好，而且在销售领域与人建立联系方面表现也很出色。

219
00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:18.550
Yeah, he's a really good salesman.
没错，他确实是个非常优秀的销售员。

220
00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:21.070
Here's a little interesting tidbit, Jenna.
这里有个有趣的小花絮，珍娜。

221
00:13:21.370 --> 00:13:26.250
There was a scene that was deleted right after this phone call of David Wallace and Michael.
在大卫·华莱士和迈克尔通完电话后，有一段被删减的戏。

222
00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:30.750
David Wallace calls Jim and tells Jim the same thing.
大卫·华莱士给吉姆打了电话，告诉了他同样的事情。

223
00:13:31.190 --> 00:13:32.770
I saw that in the script.
我在剧本里看到了。

224
00:13:32.770 --> 00:13:34.670
And they chose not to use it.
但他们选择不用那段。

225
00:13:34.730 --> 00:13:37.690
They chose to like reveal it later that it's Jim calling.
他们选择稍后再揭晓是吉姆也接到了电话。

226
00:13:37.850 --> 00:13:39.790
It's Jim being more proactive in his life.
这体现了吉姆在生活中更加主动。

227
00:13:40.070 --> 00:13:42.910
But there was a version where David calls Jim.
但确实存在一个大卫给吉姆打电话的版本。

228
00:13:43.630 --> 00:13:49.410
Yeah, I noticed that in the script because that scene on the beach with Jim and Karen
是啊，我在剧本里注意到了，因为吉姆和凯伦在海滩上

229
00:13:50.270 --> 00:13:54.190
calling David and kind of asking if they could go for the job as well,
给大卫打电话，询问他们是否也能争取那个职位的那场戏，

230
00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:56.110
that was not in the original script.
那并不在最初的剧本里。

231
00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:59.830
So that must have been something that they added later.
所以那一定是他们后来加进去的。

232
00:14:00.430 --> 00:14:05.210
Well, I remember when we were at the beach, there were a few scenes that sort of happened on the fly.
我记得我们在海滩时，确实有几场戏是临时起意拍的。

233
00:14:05.210 --> 00:14:07.130
They were like kind of pitched in the moment.
就像是当时现场想出来的点子。

234
00:14:07.390 --> 00:14:08.670
And that was one of them.
那场戏就是其中之一。

235
00:14:09.590 --> 00:14:09.730
Yeah.
没错。

236
00:14:09.950 --> 00:14:11.710
So now it is beach day.
现在是海滩日了。

237
00:14:11.930 --> 00:14:16.730
And at two minutes, 28 seconds, you get a fantastic look at Michael's outfit.
在两分二十八秒时，你可以清楚地看到迈克尔的装扮。

238
00:14:16.930 --> 00:14:21.550
He clearly bought everything he could at Sandals in the gift shop.
他显然把凉鞋度假村礼品店里能买的东西全买了。

239
00:14:21.550 --> 00:14:22.950
He's got a t-shirt.
他穿了件T恤。

240
00:14:23.070 --> 00:14:23.730
He's got a hat.
戴了顶帽子。

241
00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:27.530
He has a necklace that he probably bought there, a little puka shell necklace.
他还戴了条项链，可能是那儿买的，一条小贝壳项链。

242
00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:28.910
He's really ready.
他真是准备充分。

243
00:14:29.290 --> 00:14:33.930
And he tells everyone they better go potty before they leave.
他还告诉大家出发前最好先去上个厕所。

244
00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:36.230
I'm just like, Michael's so ready to be a dad.
我当时就想，迈克尔真是太适合当爸爸了。

245
00:14:36.230 --> 00:14:39.270
You know, he just is, bless his heart.
你知道，他就是那样，真是个可怜的好人。

246
00:14:39.590 --> 00:14:42.690
And you get to see everybody in casual attire.
而且你可以看到大家穿的都是休闲装。

247
00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:44.530
And I just loved it.
我太喜欢了。

248
00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:48.230
Well, I noticed something about our wardrobe.
我注意到了我们服装方面的一些细节。

249
00:14:48.850 --> 00:14:49.670
What's that?
什么细节？

250
00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:52.630
Do you see how many layers we all have on?
你注意到我们每个人身上穿了多少层衣服吗？

251
00:14:52.810 --> 00:14:54.790
Oh, well, that was for a reason.
哦，好吧，那是有原因的。

252
00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:55.870
Yep.
没错。

253
00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:59.730
It was because we shot this episode in March.
那是因为我们是在三月份拍的这一集。

254
00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:06.010
And we knew that that location was going to be perfectly warm and lovely during the day.
我们当时以为那个外景地白天会非常温暖宜人。

255
00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:08.050
It turned out to be hot as F.
结果热得要命。

256
00:15:08.430 --> 00:15:09.410
There was a heat wave.
当时正好赶上热浪。

257
00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:13.330
There was this crazy freak March heat wave.
三月份居然出现了那种疯狂的极端热浪。

258
00:15:13.490 --> 00:15:15.150
It was 98 degrees.
气温高达98华氏度（约37摄氏度）。

259
00:15:15.310 --> 00:15:19.350
I wrote in my journal that it was 98 degrees our first day.
我在日记里写了，我们第一天的时候气温是98度。

260
00:15:20.190 --> 00:15:20.430
Yeah.
是啊。

261
00:15:20.770 --> 00:15:25.310
But then when the sun went down, it was freezing cold.
但太阳一下山，就冷得要命。

262
00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:25.590
Yeah.
没错。

263
00:15:25.710 --> 00:15:26.730
We are in this valley.
我们当时身处山谷之中。

264
00:15:26.730 --> 00:15:30.130
So it was like these really huge swings of temperature.
所以温差非常大。

265
00:15:31.170 --> 00:15:31.330
Yeah.
确实。

266
00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:37.850
So wardrobe tried to give us layers to help take us between these two extreme temperatures.
所以服装组尽量给我们准备了多层穿搭，好让我们能适应这两种极端气温。

267
00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:41.930
And I noticed when I was looking at everyone's beach attire.
我观察大家的海滩装束时注意到了这一点。

268
00:15:42.710 --> 00:15:43.630
Their layers.
他们的叠穿。

269
00:15:43.970 --> 00:15:45.170
Well, you know what I noticed?
嗯，你知道我注意到了什么吗？

270
00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:47.310
This is really, really fun to me.
我觉得这真的、真的很有趣。

271
00:15:47.810 --> 00:15:50.390
Toby and Angela almost have on the same outfit.
托比和安吉拉穿的衣服几乎一模一样。

272
00:15:51.030 --> 00:15:51.530
What?
什么？

273
00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:53.010
I did not notice that.
我没注意到。

274
00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:55.230
They're both wearing these beige tops.
他们都穿着米色的上衣。

275
00:15:55.230 --> 00:16:00.410
They have huge beige floppy hats and like sort of like just like pants on.
他们都戴着巨大的米色软檐帽，还穿着那种裤子。

276
00:16:00.570 --> 00:16:03.130
But like and they're also both obsessed with sunscreen.
而且他们俩都对防晒霜有种执念。

277
00:16:03.350 --> 00:16:04.110
Both of them.
他们俩都是。

278
00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:10.190
Well, I noticed that both Andy and Stanley are wearing a red polo shirt.
嗯，我注意到安迪和斯坦利都穿着红色的Polo衫。

279
00:16:10.430 --> 00:16:10.850
Oh.
哦。

280
00:16:11.510 --> 00:16:11.930
Yeah.
是啊。

281
00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:13.170
I did not catch that.
我没发现这一点。

282
00:16:13.350 --> 00:16:15.050
But guess what I did catch?
但你猜我发现了什么？

283
00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:17.810
And I know it's your favorite moment of the day.
我知道这是你一天中最喜欢的时刻。

284
00:16:18.530 --> 00:16:20.610
Is it a new plant at reception?
是前台又换新植物了吗？

285
00:16:20.610 --> 00:16:22.310
Because we need a sting for this.
因为我们需要为此配个音效。

286
00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:24.190
New plant alert.
新植物警报。

287
00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:25.430
Three minutes.
三分钟。

288
00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:26.450
32 seconds.
32秒。

289
00:16:26.750 --> 00:16:29.510
It is a red flowering plant.
那是一株开红花的植物。

290
00:16:30.250 --> 00:16:34.150
There are big red flowers on this plant.
这株植物上开着大大的红花。

291
00:16:34.690 --> 00:16:35.270
Okay.
好吧。

292
00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:36.070
I'm sorry.
抱歉。

293
00:16:37.010 --> 00:16:41.550
I see that as a metaphor because Pam is going to bloom in this episode.
我觉得这是一种隐喻，因为帕姆在这一集里要绽放了。

294
00:16:41.710 --> 00:16:42.610
That is so good.
这太棒了。

295
00:16:42.830 --> 00:16:43.770
Pam's going to flower.
帕姆要开花了。

296
00:16:43.970 --> 00:16:44.950
She's going to bloom.
她要绽放了。

297
00:16:45.530 --> 00:16:50.130
Well, we forgot to talk about how Michael has told Toby he can't come on the beach day.
对了，我们忘了聊迈克尔告诉托比他不能参加海滩日的事了。

298
00:16:50.370 --> 00:16:51.310
Oh, that was so good.
哦，那段太精彩了。

299
00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:52.590
Toby's disappointment.
托比那副失望的样子。

300
00:16:52.810 --> 00:16:54.870
He doesn't get to see you in a two piece.
他没能看到你穿比基尼的样子。

301
00:16:55.290 --> 00:16:58.330
Jenna, I wrote, how did you get through that scene without laughing?
珍娜，我写道，你是怎么忍住没笑场拍完那场戏的？

302
00:16:58.850 --> 00:17:00.070
How did you do it?
你是怎么做到的？

303
00:17:00.170 --> 00:17:02.870
Because when you were like, oh, thanks, I'm going to wear my two piece.
因为当时你说，哦，谢谢，我要穿我的两件套泳衣。

304
00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:05.900
Good.
很好。

305
00:17:06.540 --> 00:17:07.600
It's so funny.
太搞笑了。

306
00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:11.480
We also didn't mention, and I'm tracking this, the Meredith flash count.
我们还没提到，我一直在留意，就是梅雷迪思的闪露次数。

307
00:17:11.720 --> 00:17:13.220
This is flash number two.
这是第二次闪露。

308
00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:19.200
Yes, Meredith has that great talking head where she explains that she wore her bathing suit to work.
没错，梅雷迪思有个很棒的访谈镜头，她解释说她穿着泳衣来上班。

309
00:17:19.340 --> 00:17:19.500
Yeah.
是啊。

310
00:17:19.820 --> 00:17:24.480
And she lifts her shirt to show you, and she's like, oh, shoot, it's in my purse.
然后她掀起衬衫给你看，结果她说，噢，糟了，泳衣在包里。

311
00:17:25.360 --> 00:17:28.840
Michael has a special assignment for Pam on beach day.
迈克尔给帕姆在海滩日安排了一个特殊任务。

312
00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:30.640
Of course, because she needs an assignment.
当然了，因为她需要一个任务。

313
00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:31.820
Yeah.
是啊。

314
00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:38.500
Michael tells her that she has to take notes all day on people's character, humor, charisma,
迈克尔让她整天记录下人们的性格、幽默感、个人魅力，

315
00:17:38.780 --> 00:17:41.780
and the indefinable qualities that they possess.
以及他们所具备的那些难以言喻的特质。

316
00:17:41.780 --> 00:17:45.220
Yes, but not their hotness per se.
没错，但本质上不是指他们的性感程度。

317
00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:47.180
I loved that line.
我喜欢那句台词。

318
00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:47.620
Yeah.
是啊。

319
00:17:48.580 --> 00:17:49.080
Per se.
本质上。

320
00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:54.340
Like, she could add that if she wanted, but it's not 100% necessary, I guess.
就像是，如果她想的话可以加上那点，但我猜那不是百分之百必要的。

321
00:17:54.500 --> 00:17:57.180
But he also doesn't tell her why she has to do this.
但他也没告诉她为什么要这么做。

322
00:17:57.380 --> 00:18:02.520
No, he just needs to find out which of his employees has the skills to be a chicken with a head.
没有，他只是需要找出他的员工里谁有能力做一只带头的鸡。

323
00:18:03.360 --> 00:18:04.060
That's right.
没错。

324
00:18:04.220 --> 00:18:05.040
That makes sense.
这说得通。

325
00:18:05.580 --> 00:18:06.040
What?
什么？

326
00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:09.760
Well, let's see.
嗯，让我们看看。

327
00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:12.880
Now the gang is all going to board the bus.
现在大家都要上车了。

328
00:18:13.180 --> 00:18:16.800
Michael's got a big party bus to take everyone to the beach.
迈克尔租了一辆大型派对巴士带大家去海滩。

329
00:18:17.060 --> 00:18:17.260
Yeah.
是啊。

330
00:18:17.480 --> 00:18:18.780
Creed's got a boogie board.
克里德带了个冲浪板。

331
00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:20.240
He's ready to go.
他准备好出发了。

332
00:18:21.260 --> 00:18:21.620
Yeah.
是啊。

333
00:18:21.780 --> 00:18:24.800
Kevin says he just wants to lay on the beach and eat hot dogs.
凯文说他只想躺在沙滩上吃热狗。

334
00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:26.620
It's literally all he's ever wanted.
这简直是他一直以来的愿望。

335
00:18:29.260 --> 00:18:33.640
Now, this bus that we're all riding in, we really rode in that.
现在，我们大家坐的这辆巴士，我们真的是坐它去的。

336
00:18:33.860 --> 00:18:35.720
That's how we got to the beach.
我们就是那样去的海滩。

337
00:18:35.960 --> 00:18:38.280
That day, but then the days after that, we drove ourselves.
那天是这样，但之后几天，我们都是自己开车去的。

338
00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:39.300
Yes.
是的。

339
00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:44.080
That first day, though, they shuttled us from the stages, and the plan was that we would use the time
不过第一天，他们用班车把我们从摄影棚接过去，计划是利用这段时间

340
00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:45.680
to shoot the scene with Michael.
去拍迈克尔的那场戏。

341
00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:49.180
But then we had extra time.
但后来我们有了额外的时间。

342
00:18:49.740 --> 00:18:50.220
Yeah.
是啊。

343
00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:52.380
So we all started singing.
所以我们就开始唱歌了。

344
00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:56.720
Well, listen, I watched the DVD commentary for this, and I thought it was so sweet because
听着，我看了这集的DVD评论音轨，我觉得特别温馨，因为

345
00:18:56.720 --> 00:19:01.300
Harold Ramis is on the DVD commentary, and Jen Salata was on it as well.
哈罗德·雷米斯在评论音轨里，简·萨拉塔也在。

346
00:19:01.400 --> 00:19:05.780
And Jen was like, you know, Harold said, I'd never been on a bus where we didn't sing.
简说，你知道吗，哈罗德说，我从没坐过不唱歌的巴士。

347
00:19:07.480 --> 00:19:07.880
Yes.
是的。

348
00:19:07.880 --> 00:19:11.240
He was like, when you get on a bus with people, you sing, right?
他说，当你和大家一起坐巴士时，你们会唱歌，对吧？

349
00:19:11.420 --> 00:19:13.800
That was Harold Ramis, Being Genius.
那就是哈罗德·雷米斯，天才的体现。

350
00:19:14.780 --> 00:19:17.440
And I do know two other songs we sang, Jenna.
而且我知道我们还唱了另外两首歌，珍娜。

351
00:19:17.980 --> 00:19:18.700
Do you remember?
你还记得吗？

352
00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:19.840
What?
什么？

353
00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:21.400
We sang Tiny Dancer.
我们唱了《Tiny Dancer》。

354
00:19:22.100 --> 00:19:23.000
Oh, yes.
噢，记得。

355
00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:26.420
And then we also sang Friends, and we did the hand claps.
然后我们还唱了《Friends》，还拍手伴奏了。

356
00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:28.060
I remember that.
我记得那个。

357
00:19:28.240 --> 00:19:28.440
Yeah.
是啊。

358
00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:31.180
I didn't know all the words to that, so I just clapped.
我没记住所有的歌词，所以就只负责拍手。

359
00:19:31.700 --> 00:19:37.760
Well, Angela, I have to say, I remember that vibe of us just singing our way to the beach
安吉拉，我得说，我记得那种感觉，我们一路唱着歌去海滩，

360
00:19:37.760 --> 00:19:39.300
that very first day.
就在第一天。

361
00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:45.820
And then, frankly, as we walked off the bus and got hit in the face with that 98-degree weather,
然后，坦白说，当我们走下大巴，被那98华氏度（约37摄氏度）的高温扑面而来时，

362
00:19:46.540 --> 00:19:50.540
I feel like you see that play out on our faces.
我觉得你们能从我们的表情里看出来。

363
00:19:51.120 --> 00:19:53.100
I felt the same thing in re-watching this.
重温这一段时，我也有同感。

364
00:19:53.100 --> 00:19:56.640
That shot, when we're walking across the beach, we're all carrying our stuff.
那个镜头，我们走过海滩，每个人都扛着东西。

365
00:19:57.040 --> 00:19:58.640
Meredith is dragging a cooler.
梅雷迪思拖着一个冷藏箱。

366
00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:00.300
Do you know the shot I'm talking about?
你知道我说的是哪个镜头吗？

367
00:20:00.520 --> 00:20:00.760
Yes.
知道。

368
00:20:00.760 --> 00:20:03.400
I took a photo of it because I think it's so amazing.
我拍了张照，因为我觉得那太棒了。

369
00:20:03.620 --> 00:20:04.420
I took a screen grab.
我截了屏。

370
00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:06.600
That is all real.
那都是真实的。

371
00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:12.380
Like, that struggle to drag that stuff across the beach, and it was hot.
就像那种在海滩上拖着东西的挣扎，而且天气很热。

372
00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:14.000
You guys have all been there.
你们肯定都有过这种经历。

373
00:20:14.220 --> 00:20:19.460
You've been to that beach where you had to park super far away and lug your stuff in the heat,
你们肯定去过那种需要把车停得特别远，然后在高温下扛着东西走过去的海滩，

374
00:20:19.460 --> 00:20:25.400
and all your enthusiasm of getting there is so quickly like, oh, God, here we go.
刚到时的那种兴奋劲儿很快就变成了，天哪，又来了。

375
00:20:26.020 --> 00:20:30.720
Well, Angela, you mentioned a little bit about the challenges of this location,
安吉拉，你刚才稍微提到了这个外景地的挑战，

376
00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:33.100
and Jen talked about that as well.
简也谈到了这一点。

377
00:20:33.340 --> 00:20:36.600
So, Sam, will you play that audio clip number eight?
那么，山姆，能播放一下八号音频片段吗？

378
00:20:36.760 --> 00:20:39.460
It was a super fun episode, but it was really challenging.
那是一集非常有趣的剧集，但拍摄过程确实充满挑战。

379
00:20:39.740 --> 00:20:42.520
I remember even the bathrooms weren't close to the location,
我记得连洗手间离拍摄地都不近，

380
00:20:42.840 --> 00:20:45.960
and they were as close as they could be, but we needed a golf cart,
虽然已经是能安排到的最近距离了，但我们还是需要高尔夫球车，

381
00:20:45.960 --> 00:20:50.220
and so anytime anybody had to go to the bathroom, there was a golf cart taking people up a hill,
所以每次有人要上厕所，都得有高尔夫球车把人送上山坡，

382
00:20:50.240 --> 00:20:54.420
and I remember it was super hot and super cold all within the course of one day.
我记得那一天里天气忽冷忽热的。

383
00:20:54.420 --> 00:21:00.400
There were heat lamps and hand warmers, and then it was really, really hot in the middle of the afternoon.
一会儿要用加热灯和暖宝宝，到了下午又变得非常非常热。

384
00:21:00.800 --> 00:21:07.120
So everything about this episode was a challenge, and I feel like we just,
所以这一集的方方面面都是挑战，我觉得我们为了，

385
00:21:07.340 --> 00:21:10.100
in order to get everything great, it just took a long time.
为了把一切拍好，真的花了很多时间。

386
00:21:10.540 --> 00:21:11.180
Oh, yeah.
噢，没错。

387
00:21:11.320 --> 00:21:13.200
It was slim pickings out there on the beach.
海滩上几乎没什么可用的地方。

388
00:21:13.320 --> 00:21:15.740
I mean, we had just enough room to shoot.
我是说，我们只有刚好够用的空间来拍摄。

389
00:21:16.340 --> 00:21:23.320
There was, I mean, I remember us just being huddled under, like, a little pop-up tent or something.
我记得我们当时就挤在一个小小的遮阳棚之类的东西下面。

390
00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:28.900
Yeah, I mean, when I was looking at the photos that I have, there's nothing out there.
是啊，当我翻看我有的那些照片时，外面什么都没有。

391
00:21:29.080 --> 00:21:33.200
Once we were on the beach, we were sort of on our own, because things couldn't be in the shot.
一旦到了海滩上，我们就只能靠自己了，因为东西不能入镜。

392
00:21:33.420 --> 00:21:35.180
They did get us, like, some lawn chairs.
他们确实给我们弄了一些折叠椅。

393
00:21:35.340 --> 00:21:39.820
I have this great photo, Jenna, of Steve and I sitting next to each other, clearly between a scene,
珍娜，我有一张很棒的照片，是史蒂夫和我坐在一起，显然是在两场戏之间，

394
00:21:39.820 --> 00:21:43.460
and we're just, like, in lawn chairs, like, just hanging out and talking.
我们坐在折叠椅上，闲聊着。

395
00:21:43.460 --> 00:21:44.500
I just love it.
我太喜欢那张照片了。

396
00:21:45.420 --> 00:21:48.020
And also this week, Jenna, I brought my football.
还有这周，珍娜，我带了我的橄榄球。

397
00:21:49.360 --> 00:21:49.720
Okay.
好的。

398
00:21:49.720 --> 00:21:51.540
Like, in real life, I had it in my car.
在现实生活中，我把它放在车里。

399
00:21:51.540 --> 00:21:57.740
And when they would have some setups to do, in particular, like, when they were setting up the hot dogs and all that,
当他们需要布置场景时，特别是布置热狗摊那些东西的时候，

400
00:21:57.900 --> 00:22:01.180
I have these photos of us throwing the football on the beach.
我还有这些我们在海滩上扔橄榄球的照片。

401
00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:02.040
Aw.
噢。

402
00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:03.820
We were having our own beach day.
我们当时在过我们自己的海滩日。

403
00:22:03.820 --> 00:22:10.100
So, yes, it was sort of, like, there weren't bathrooms nearby, and it was hot, but we were also just making our own fun.
所以，没错，当时的情况是，附近没有洗手间，天气也很热，但我们也在自娱自乐。

404
00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:11.380
And we always did that.
我们一直都是这样。

405
00:22:11.380 --> 00:22:16.500
I felt like as a group, Jenna, we were really just kind of roll with it and make the best of it.
我觉得作为一个团队，珍娜，我们真的很随遇而安，尽量把事情做到最好。

406
00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:18.500
And I always love that about our show.
我一直很喜欢我们剧组这一点。

407
00:22:18.940 --> 00:22:19.500
All right.
好吧。

408
00:22:19.580 --> 00:22:20.200
Well, let's see.
嗯，让我们看看。

409
00:22:20.260 --> 00:22:21.140
Where are we?
我们到哪儿了？

410
00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:22.440
We've arrived.
我们到了。

411
00:22:22.440 --> 00:22:24.560
We're yanking our stuff down the beach.
我们正把东西拖下海滩。

412
00:22:24.680 --> 00:22:28.380
And Michael tells us it's time to start the fun-tivities.
迈克尔告诉我们该开始“欢乐活动”了。

413
00:22:28.820 --> 00:22:29.900
Such a great word.
真是个好词。

414
00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:31.200
I want to use it.
我想用用看。

415
00:22:31.300 --> 00:22:33.480
It's like nifty-gifty's fun-tivities.
就像那种“精巧礼物”的“欢乐活动”。

416
00:22:33.820 --> 00:22:37.300
It's like, can you imagine if I did that, like, at a family reunion?
就像，你能想象如果我在家庭聚会上这么说吗？

417
00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:39.680
All right, everybody, it's time for fun-tivities.
好了，各位，欢乐活动时间到了。

418
00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:41.600
I love it.
我喜欢。

419
00:22:41.880 --> 00:22:42.520
I know.
我知道。

420
00:22:42.800 --> 00:22:42.940
Personally.
就我个人而言。

421
00:22:43.220 --> 00:22:44.620
You and I would both be excited.
你和我都会很兴奋的。

422
00:22:44.620 --> 00:22:47.340
We'd want to be on the fun-tivities, like, organizing group.
我们会想加入欢乐活动，比如，组织小组。

423
00:22:47.780 --> 00:22:49.180
The fun-tivities committee.
欢乐活动委员会。

424
00:22:49.400 --> 00:22:50.680
The fun-tivities committee.
欢乐活动委员会。

425
00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:52.900
Or the committee to plan fun-tivities.
或者欢乐活动策划委员会。

426
00:22:53.240 --> 00:22:54.740
Or what was wrong with my brain?
还是说我的脑子出了什么问题？

427
00:22:54.820 --> 00:22:56.500
The fun-tivities organizing group.
欢乐活动组织小组。

428
00:22:56.780 --> 00:22:56.960
What?
什么？

429
00:22:59.520 --> 00:23:01.140
Mama needs another cup of tea.
老娘需要再来杯茶。

430
00:23:02.760 --> 00:23:06.540
Well, Michael tells everyone that they have to divide into teaMs.
嗯，迈克尔告诉大家他们必须分成小组。

431
00:23:06.860 --> 00:23:11.000
He picks the team leaders, quote-unquote, randomly.
他“随机”挑选了队长。

432
00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:13.580
They are Jim, Dwight, Andy, and Stanley.
他们是吉姆、德怀特、安迪和斯坦利。

433
00:23:13.920 --> 00:23:19.260
These guys are clearly his top contenders for the job.
这些人显然是他心目中这份工作的头号竞争者。

434
00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:22.180
And he makes them choose their tribes.
然后他让他们挑选各自的部落。

435
00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:22.900
Yes.
没错。

436
00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:23.560
But not Pam.
但帕姆不行。

437
00:23:23.900 --> 00:23:25.040
Pam cannot be included.
帕姆不能被包括在内。

